<BlogPost slug="iptv-vs-cable-canada">
  <p>IPTV vs cable in Canada is a common question because many Canadian households pay for a cable or satellite bundle and watch only a handful of channels. Here is how IPTV compares for a typical home in Canada. For the full feature table, see our <a href="/blog/iptv-vs-cable/">general IPTV vs cable guide</a>.</p>

  <h2>Cost and contracts</h2>
  <p>Cable bundles often carry equipment fees and a term agreement, and prices rise after promotions end. Prime IPTV publishes its prices, has <a href="/pricing/">no contract</a> and offers cancellation. Compare against your own bill, including taxes and box rental.</p>

  <h2>Channels and sports</h2>
  <p>Cable offers fixed packages, and sports often sit in extra tiers. Prime IPTV includes Canadian and US channels, channels from other countries, Canadian, US and international sports, and PPV in 4K and FHD. We do not publish a channel list, so use the free trial to confirm the ones you need.</p>

  <h2>Equipment and screens</h2>
  <p>Cable usually means a box per TV. IPTV runs on a Smart TV, a Firestick, an Android box, an Apple TV, a phone or a computer with a compatible app. Plans cover 1 to 4 connections.</p>

  <h2>Your internet matters</h2>
  <p>IPTV depends on your home internet. Check your speed against the <a href="/iptv-internet-speed-requirements/">speed guide</a>. A wired connection helps most.</p>

  <h2>Switching without risk</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Keep your cable while you test.</li>
    <li>Start the <a href="/free-trial/">free trial</a> and test your must-have channels and a live game.</li>
    <li>Choose a plan only if the trial works on your connection.</li>
    <li>Cancel cable on your own timeline, and check any cancellation terms first.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Quick answers</h2>
  <h3>Do I still need cable internet?</h3>
  <p>You need home internet, but not a cable TV package.</p>
  <h3>Can I watch outside the home?</h3>
  <p>Yes, on compatible phone and tablet apps, within your connection limit.</p>
</BlogPost>
